From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU interpreter: per-pair step for the body of a translated block

What it is for:
  Translated VU code ran one function per instruction pair, each called by
  the run loop (budget and stop checks, a table lookup, an indirect call),
  and each ended with the PC update, the branch-delay handling and the
  E/D/T end checks. In a straight-line run of pairs none of that tail can
  do anything but advance the PC.

The change (used only by translated code; the interpreter is unchanged):
  - execPairWith takes `blockBody`: for a pair in the body of a translated
    block (no branch, no E/D/T bit, nothing pending), after the writes and
    the VF0/VI0 reset it only advances the cycle and returns; the block
    sets the PC itself.
  - VU1NativeAccess::blockPairUpper / blockPairUpperLower: one body pair
    at a constant PC: the stop request check, then the step with
    blockBody. The run loop's other checks cannot fire inside a block: the
    previous pair's cycle advance has just made every commit due by this
    cycle, the step itself makes the budget check, and the PC is inside
    code memory. The PC is set only when the block leaves early (only
    branches read it). The first pair skips the stop check too (the run
    loop has just made it).
  - MaskedTiming / calculatePairReadyCycleMasked: the constant hazard
    check with only the read lanes a block's translator could not settle
    at build time (a lane whose latest write is by an earlier pair of the
    block at least that write's latency in pairs back cannot stall, since
    pairs issue at least one cycle apart). A static_assert keeps the masks
    subsets of what the pair reads.
  - VU1NativeAccess::blockCanEnter (no branch, delay slot or end pending),
    blockSetPc and loopHead, for the block functions the port generates.
  A block function runs its body pairs this way and its last pair through
  the ordinary step, so the run loop sees the same state after it as after
  running the same pairs one by one.

How it was checked:
  The port's replay: same checksums and cycle counts on the 40 VU1 and 60
  VU0 workloads with blocks on, with them off and interpreted. ps2xTest
  454/454.

